<title>USA: Steelworkers win court unemployment benefit ruling.</title>
<headline>Steelworkers win court unemployment benefit ruling.</headline>
<dateline>PITTSBURGH 1997-03-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>The United Steelworkers of America said on Wednesday they won the latest round of a legal battle over the right of striking Wheeling-Pittsburgh Steel employees to unemployment compensation.</p>
<p>Wheeling-Pittsburgh's parent is WHX Corp.</p>
<p>The union said a Belmont County Common Pleas Court judge ruled that the workers were entitled to unemployment benefits because the company was refusing to negotiate over a defined-benefit pension plan.</p>
<p>But the union noted that Wheeling-Pittsburgh took its case to an appellate panel, which has given each side until Friday to submit briefs.</p>
<p>Claims for unemployment benefits for striking Wheeling-Pittsburgh employees in Pennsylvania and West Virginia have been denied, but the union is appealing the decisions in both states, it said.</p>
<p>Wheeling-Pittsburgh officials were not immediately available for comment.</p>
</text>
